        <technology>
            <technologyDescriptionAndIncludedProcesses>During milling the cloth undergoes repeated intermittent compression in the various parts of the machine, but after each compression, fabric is allowed to recover. Some of the features of modemmachines include plastic milling rollers, chain drives, hydraulic control of pressure on milling rollers and spout, stainless steel and plastic parts, and large capacity units (Whewell, 1977). Some goods, such as felt hats and blankets, are milled under acidic conditions (pH 3)  using dilute sulphuric acids. Acid milling is especially suitable for dyed goods, as many of the dyes applied on wool have poor resistance to neutral and alkaline treatments. It is more preferable since there is less fibre degradation and minimised washing off, resulting in lower water consumption (Choudhury, 2017). 

In this model, the rinsing and hydroextracting/drying process is not covered. The activity starts when the raw materials enter the process. Materials entering the process  are included as well as energy uses and infrastructure. There is no direct emission to air since the emission comes from electricity usage, while the emission to water comes from wastewater and waste lube oil. The amount of wastewater assumed same as water input, no water uptakes from wet fabrics input.  In the model, the finishing process is not included in the scope of this dataset and enegy sources of machines used solely from electricity.  The consumption of lubricating oil using a thermobonding oven as a proxy.


Whewell, C. S. 1977. The Chemistry of Wool Finishing. In Chemistry of Natural Protein Fibers, pp 333–370. New York : Plenum Press
Choudhury, Asum Kumar Roy. 2017. Wool and silk finishing processes. In Principle of textile Finishing, pp 417-466. United Kingdom: Woodhead Publishing.</technologyDescriptionAndIncludedProcesses>
            <technologicalApplicability>na</technologicalApplicability>
